    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right to be skeptical, and I apologize if my previous attempt to explain the concept using a metaphor about \"gobbling up\" data and \"confused elves\" felt too whimsical or distracting from the technical reality you need.\n\nThe reason your DNS changes aren't reflecting globally immediately is indeed due to **TTL (Time To Live)** values and **caching**. When you update an A record, the old DNS servers and resolvers that have already fetched that record will keep serving the old IP address until their internal cache expires based on the TTL setting.\n\nIf your previous TTL was set to a high value (like 3600 seconds or 1 hour), the \"old\" data is simply being held in memory by those servers for that specific duration. It isn't disappearing or getting stuck in transit; it is just sitting in the cache waiting for the timer to run out. Once the TTL expires, the next request from a resolver will query the authoritative nameservers again, and they will return the new IP address you just published.\n\nTo speed this up, you can try lowering the TTL on your DNS records to a lower value (like 300 seconds) before making the change, or simply",
      "source": "target",
